#
# The ANT+ compatibility profiles' policy settings.
# docs/radiant-security.md section 11.6 is the table these mirror.

menu "RadiANT profile policy"

choice
	prompt "enrol: how a new receiver gets this node's key"
	default RADIANT_ENROL_PHYSICAL
	help
	  Adding a receiver to an existing encrypted network. A user buys a
	  second head unit in March and has to be able to add it to a strap they
	  set up in January, without re-provisioning the first head unit and
	  without taking the strap apart.

	  Read this before choosing, because it is the whole trade: PAIRING
	  HAPPENS IN THE CLEAR. An active attacker present during the window can
	  be the man in the middle. The mitigation is a six-digit fingerprint a
	  human compares ACROSS TWO SCREENS - and a strap has no screen, so over
	  the air that comparison is one-sided. Out-of-band provisioning, a
	  printed key or a QR code, genuinely defeats the attack rather than
	  mitigating it, and it stays the recommended path for anything that
	  matters.

	  And the asymmetry nobody expects: RadiANT v1 can ADD a receiver in
	  sixty seconds and can REMOVE one only by re-provisioning the whole
	  network, because every keyholder holds the same root key. Per-receiver
	  revocation is out of scope. Read that sentence before choosing
	  open-window.

config RADIANT_ENROL_CLOSED
	bool "closed - no over-air enrolment, ever"
	help
	  Keys arrive only out of band: the host message set on a host-attached
	  node, or the manufacturer's provisioning path. There is no over-air
	  pairing window and no way to open one.

	  THIS IS THE RECOMMENDED SETTING FOR ANYTHING THAT MATTERS. No
	  protocol, no attack surface during pairing at all, and no dependence
	  on the user actually looking at a number. It is not the default only
	  because a sealed strap with no port has no path at all under it.

config RADIANT_ENROL_PHYSICAL
	bool "physical - a bounded window opened by a physical action (default)"
	help
	  A 60-second pairing window opened by a button, a magnet or a strap
	  re-seat at the node. One pairing per window, and a second press does
	  not extend the first.

	  The window opens on whichever channel the node is currently on - the
	  frames are additional pages, not a channel change - so every receiver
	  that is already listening keeps its stream throughout, and a node in
	  private mode does not have to leave it to gain a receiver.

	  The mitigations are the bounded window, one pairing per window, the
	  physical trigger, and optionally reduced transmit power so the
	  attacker has to be in the room. They are mitigations, not a fix: the
	  fingerprint is still one-sided on a device with no display.

config RADIANT_ENROL_OPEN_WINDOW
	bool "open-window - a keyholder may open a window with a command"
	help
	  A window a CURRENT KEYHOLDER opens with an authenticated command, for
	  a node with no button and no host. Rate-limited to one window every
	  five minutes and bounded to sixty seconds.

	  THIS IS THE WEAKEST OF THE THREE and it exists because a sealed strap
	  otherwise has no path at all. The authentication of the command is the
	  command layer's, not this module's: a node that opens a window on an
	  unverified command has built "anybody may enrol against me".

	  Combined with the paragraph above about removal - add in sixty
	  seconds, remove only by re-provisioning - this is the setting that
	  most deserves a second reading before a product ships with it.

endchoice

endmenu
